{
  "teamId": 108,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 Los Angeles Angels were a team defined by their limitations from the very first week. With an aging and injury-prone roster, a thin rotation, and a bullpen that consistently squandered leads, the Angels never found the sustained momentum needed to compete in a brutal AL West. They finished 63-99, one of the worst records in baseball, and missed the playoffs for the ninth consecutive season — a franchise drought that has become its own kind of story.\n\nApril offered a flicker of hope, as Mike Trout delivered a vintage blast and Taylor Ward chipped in clutch hits, but the bullpen — led by closer Carlos Estévez in his worst moments — unraveled several games before they could be sealed. The pattern was established early: the Angels would scratch and claw to put runs on the board, only to watch the lead evaporate in the late innings. Estévez alone appeared in multiple high-leverage disasters during the first two months, and his blown saves became a symbol of the team's inability to close out close games.\n\nThe summer months brought the season's most interesting subplot: the unexpected emergence of a group of younger contributors. Logan O'Hoppe established himself as a legitimate offensive catcher, Jo Adell flashed the power that once made him a top prospect, and Zach Neto took strides toward becoming a cornerstone shortstop. Taylor Ward's grand slam in late July and Willie Calhoun's go-ahead shot in July provided genuine thrills, and for a stretch the Angels played competitive baseball. June was actually the team's best month at 15-11, a remarkable interlude in an otherwise bleak season.\n\nBut the second half was a collapse. August brought a 9-19 record and September was even worse at 7-19, as the pitching staff wore down and the offense regressed. Rookies and depth pieces littered the mound in critical moments, and the results were predictable. The Angels closed out the year by getting walked off in Texas, a fitting and deflating end to a season that offered just enough brightness to make the darkness more frustrating. The roster rebuild is underway, but 2024 made clear that the Angels remain years away from contention.",
  "chapters": [
